content.window_manager_marco_and_compiz:
Window management is handled by default by Marco, MATE's native window manager forked from Metacity. Marco offers flexible compositing engines:

- Software rendering without compositing for minimal resource usage;
- Integrated software compositing via XRender;
- Hardware-accelerated compositing utilizing OpenGL and XPresent to prevent screen tearing during video playback and desktop navigation.

For advanced visual effects, Ubuntu MATE retains native integration with Compiz, enabling users to enable 3D desktop cube workspaces and fluid window animations via MATE Tweak.

content.mate_tweak_and_desktop_layouts:
A distinctive hallmark of Ubuntu MATE is `MATE Tweak`, a specialized administrative utility that allows instantaneous switching between preset desktop workflows:

- Traditional: The classic two-panel GNOME 2 layout with Applications/Places/System menus;
- Cupertino: A macOS-styled layout with a top global bar and bottom dock;
- Mutiny: A left-docked vertical panel reminiscent of Ubuntu's Unity desktop;
- Redmond: A single bottom taskbar with start menu similar to classic Windows;
- Contemporary & Pantheon: Modern, streamlined layouts optimized for widescreen displays.

MATE Tweak also controls window titlebar button placement (left or right) and desktop icon visibility.

content.file_manager_caja_and_core_tools:
System file navigation is provided by Caja (fork of Nautilus 2). Caja features tabbed browsing, split-view vertical pane mode (F3), folder color emblems, and network share mounting (SSH, FTP, WebDAV, Samba). The default desktop utilities include:

- Pluma: Flexible text editor with syntax highlighting and plugin architecture;
- Atril: Universal document viewer supporting PDF, PostScript, DJVU, and EPUB formats;
- Eye of MATE (eom): Fast image viewer with image metadata inspection;
- Engrampa: Archive manager supporting tar, zip, 7z, and rar formats;
- MATE Terminal: Tabbed terminal emulator with color profile support.

content.hardware_support_and_architectures:
Ubuntu MATE targets the 64-bit `x86_64` (amd64) architecture for its desktop PC installation media, having retired 32-bit (i386) ISOs in 2019. System hardware requirements include:

- Processor: 64-bit dual-core CPU (2.0 GHz or faster recommended);
- Memory (RAM): Minimum 2 GB RAM (4 GB recommended for modern web multitasking);
- Storage: 15 GB of available disk space (25 GB recommended);
- Display: 1024x768 resolution minimum.

In addition to x86_64 PC hardware, Ubuntu MATE maintains dedicated official images tailored for Raspberry Pi single-board computers (ARM architectures `armhf` and `arm64`).
